  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1044 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe main concern in the near term forecast will be the potential for \na prolonged period of rainfall across areas with fairly saturated \nsoil. The remnants of what used to be Hurricane Polo will continue \nto tap into warm, moist Gulf air as it lifts to the northeast \nthrough the overnight hours and into Thursday. With Pwat values of \n1.8\"+ and a skinny sounding, expect efficient rainfall overnight and \ninto tomorrow. Recent trends in models have shown the main area of \nrainfall shifting north, closer to the I-80 corridor. By the time \nall is said and done with the rainfall, we are still looking at 2-4\" \nfor many areas and localized 5\"+ in other areas. Flash flooding will \nbe possible, as as a result, the Flood Watch will remain in effect \nthrough Thursday evening. Instability will remain limited \novernight; however, with a boundary cutting across eastern \nNebraska and decent shear, can't entirely rule out a quick spin \nup or two.\n\nFriday, the system moves into Missouri and we get a much needed \nbreak from the rain. Skies clear out as high pressure moves in. \nCooler temperatures (60s) will be expected for highs. \n\nQuieter weather is expected heading into the weekend and through the \nstart of next week as we come under northwest flow. Expect plenty of \nsunshine and a drier weather pattern. Weekend highs will be in the \n60s and 70s, with widespread 70s expected heading into the start of \nnext week. Lows will be in the 40s and 50s.",
